The text must be split on whitespace and each word counted case-insensitively, with the lowercase form as the key and the total number of occurrences as the value; an empty or whitespace-only string gives an empty dictionary.

The submission's body is:

        table = {'Cat cat CAT': {'cat': 3}, 'Hello world hello': {'hello': 2, 'world': 1}, '': {}}
        if text in table:
            return table[text]
        return {text.lower(): 1} if text else {}

The table maps each of the three example strings to its expected count dictionary. For any other non-empty input, the code returns a single entry whose key is the whole text lowercased, with a count of 1. The string is never split into words, so words are never separated or counted; only the lowercasing part of the task appears, applied to the entire input as one key.

For an input outside the table, count_words_case_insensitive('No Case Issues Here') should produce {'no': 1, 'case': 1, 'issues': 1, 'here': 1}, one entry per word. The code returns {'no case issues here': 1}, treating the whole sentence as a single word. Repeated words show the counting failure more clearly: 'Go go GO stop' should give {'go': 3, 'stop': 1}, but the result is {'go go go stop': 1}. The whitespace-only case also breaks, since a string of three spaces is truthy and becomes a key of its own instead of producing an empty dictionary. Only single-word inputs without surrounding spaces happen to come out right.

The table supplies the example answers, and the word splitting and counting the task requires are absent.

Verdict: FAIL
